DOS(1)                         P32W Unix                          DOS(1)

NAME
       dos, dosdir, doscp, dosrm, dosmkdir, dosformat - USB FAT files

SYNOPSIS
       dosdir [-i image] [path ...]
       doscp [-i image] [-rv] source ... target
       dosrm [-i image] [-rv] path ...
       dosmkdir [-i image] path ...
       dosformat [-i image] [-F 16|32] [-v label]

DESCRIPTION
       These read and write the FAT filesystem of a USB drive (a
       thumb drive formatted by Windows, the Mac, a camera), so
       files can go between P32W and other computers. The drive is
       not mounted: each command opens /dev/rusb (see usb(4)), does
       its work and writes everything back before it exits, so the
       drive can be pulled out once the prompt is back.

       Paths on the drive start with "::" for doscp: ::hello.c,
       ::/docs/notes.txt, and :: alone for the top directory.
       dosdir, dosrm and dosmkdir take only drive paths, so the ::
       is optional for them. Names on the drive are matched without
       regard to case, as on Windows and the Mac, and either the
       long name or the 8.3 short name will do. A path with spaces
       must be quoted.

       dosdir lists the directories (the top one if none is named):
       size or <DIR>, the date and time last written, and the name.
       The first line gives the FAT type, the volume label, the
       size and the space free.

       doscp copies from P32W to the drive or from the drive to P32W,
       never both in one command: the sources are all on one side
       and the target on the other. With one source, the target is
       the new name, or a directory to copy into; with more, it
       must be a directory. A file of the same name on the drive is
       replaced. -r copies directories and everything in them. -v
       prints each name as it is copied. Times last written are
       kept both ways. A file copied to P32W is made mode 0666 less
       the umask.

       dosrm removes files from the drive; a directory only if it
       is empty, or with -r, with everything in it.

       dosmkdir makes directories on the drive.

       dosformat makes an empty FAT filesystem on the whole drive:
       an MBR partition table with one partition from 1 MiB in (as
       flash drives come), FAT32 for 512 MB and more, FAT16 below
       (at least 3 MB), with clusters sized as Windows sizes them.
       -F chooses FAT16 (up to 2 GB) or FAT32 (33 MB and more). -v
       gives the volume label (up to 11 letters, digits, - and _).
       Everything on the drive is lost, without a question asked.

       -i image works on a disk image file instead of /dev/rusb: a
       copy of a small drive (cat /dev/rusb > image), or one the
       Mac made. An image is read up to 2 GB; the drive itself is
       read in whole sectors by number, to its end.

       dos itself only prints how to use the others: they are one
       program under five names.

FAT
       FAT12, FAT16 and FAT32 are read and written, with long names
       ("VFAT"), on a drive partitioned with an MBR or formatted
       whole ("superfloppy"). Long names are UTF-8 on P32W and
       UCS-2 on the drive. A name can hold anything but \ / : * ? "
       < > |, and cannot end in a dot or a space. A new name that
       fits 8.3 in one case is written as a short name only; others
       get a long name and a short one made from it (LONGNA~1.TXT).

       P32W's names stop at 28 characters. doscp copies a file whose
       long name is longer under its short name, and says so.

EXAMPLES
       dosdir
              what is on the drive

       doscp report.txt photo.bmp ::
              two files onto the drive's top directory

       doscp -r ::/Documents/p32w /usr/ken
              a directory tree from the drive into /usr/ken

       doscp "::My Notes.txt" notes.txt
              a file with a long name, renamed on the way

       dosformat -v BACKUP
              a new FAT32 (or FAT16) drive, labelled BACKUP

FILES
       /dev/rusb

SEE ALSO
       usb(4), tar(1), zip(1)

BUGS
       FAT keeps local times: P32W's TZ (or /etc/TZ) should be the
       other computer's, or the times are off by the difference.
       FAT's two-second times are rounded down. The Mac stores the
       time with the offset in effect when it writes, so a file
       from winter can read an hour off in summer.

       Files are copied as they are: Windows' text files keep their
       CR LF line ends. The hidden, system and read-only
       attributes are not shown or set. Names are matched without
       regard to case in ASCII only. dosformat makes no FAT12
       drives and writes no boot code.
